---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Dialog chain"
  description="How the game routes a world event to a specific line of dialog — from trigger tables through message files to the renderer."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'Message System', href: `${base}/field-guide/message-system/` },
    { label: 'Dialog chain' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    When a player talks to an NPC, enters an area, or uses an item, the
    engine has to figure out which specific line of dialog to show. That
    resolution isn't a single lookup — it's a four-step chain that starts
    with an abstract event ID and ends with rendered UTF-16-LE text on
    screen. Every piece of dialog in the game travels this chain, and
    understanding it is the key to finding where any given line lives in
    the ROM.
  </p>

  <h2>The chain at a glance</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Game event</strong> — an NPC interaction, area entry, item
      use, or scripted trigger fires an event ID.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Content-ID lookup</strong> — a 9-byte record table in
      <code>arm9.bin</code> (or an overlay) resolves the event's content
      ID to a numeric ROM file ID.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Talkstart resolution</strong> — the talkstart file at that
      file ID maps a (scenario, slot) pair to a specific message file ID
      using fixed 20-byte records.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Message file playback</strong> — the message file (a RESO
      v1.21 variant=1 container) holds the actual dialog: CMD records and
      UTF-16-LE text. Overlay 157 renders the result on screen.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Steps 2 and 3 are pure indirection — they exist so the engine can
    route thousands of distinct events to the right file using compact
    integer tables instead of path strings. The engine never references
    files by path at runtime; a grep of <code>arm9.bin</code> for path
    strings like "talkstart", "msg", or "message" returns zero matches.
    Everything is integer file IDs from the NDS FAT/FNT.
  </p>

  <h2>File-ID addressing</h2>
  <p>
    The NDS file allocation table (FAT) assigns every file in the ROM a
    sequential integer ID. The file name table (FNT) maps those IDs to
    directory paths — but only extraction tools use the FNT. The running
    game works exclusively with the integer IDs. The dialog-relevant
    ranges are:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Folder</th><th>File-ID range</th><th>Count</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>data/message/</code> (deep tree)</td><td>511 -- 1838</td><td>1,328</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>data/script/</code></td><td>2151 -- 2533</td><td>383</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>data/onemsg/</code></td><td>2122 -- 2133</td><td>12</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>data/talk/</code> (single <code>word.dat</code>)</td><td>2549</td><td>1</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>data/talkstart/</code></td><td>2550 -- 2589</td><td>40</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>data/topic/</code></td><td>2591 -- 2594</td><td>4</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Content-ID lookup tables</h2>
  <p>
    The first resolution step converts a content ID (the abstract "I want
    content X" value) into a file ID. The engine does this by scanning
    tables of fixed 9-byte records. Each record packs two (key, file_id)
    pairs:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`byte 0      = 0x00
bytes 1..2  = u16 LE key
bytes 3..4  = u16 LE file_id
bytes 5..6  = u16 LE key
bytes 7..8  = u16 LE file_id`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Eight such tables exist across the ROM — five baked into
    <code>arm9.bin</code> and three in overlays:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Location</th>
        <th>Records</th>
        <th>Key range</th>
        <th>File-ID range</th>
        <th>Folders covered</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>arm9 @ 0x7ee5f</code></td>
        <td>180</td>
        <td>0xf02e -- 0xf3c6</td>
        <td>613 -- 2642</td>
        <td>message, minigame, model, mydesign, npc, onemsg, pc_shop, recipe, script, shop, start, talkstart, topic, ucc (14 folders)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>arm9 @ 0x7c4dc</code></td>
        <td>8</td>
        <td>0x0c3a -- 0x0c76</td>
        <td>2569 -- 2642</td>
        <td>talkstart, topic, ucc</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>arm9 @ 0x7c75d</code></td>
        <td>14</td>
        <td>0x0e2e -- 0x0e79</td>
        <td>1746 -- 1898</td>
        <td>message, minigame, model</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>arm9 @ 0x7ca14</code></td>
        <td>18</td>
        <td>0x0f87 -- 0x0fb6</td>
        <td>774 -- 989</td>
        <td>message</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>arm9 @ 0x7dc42</code></td>
        <td>12</td>
        <td>0xf02c -- 0xf044</td>
        <td>339 -- 737</td>
        <td>2d, message</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ov027 @ 0x299b</code></td>
        <td>14</td>
        <td>0x00f1 -- 0x015b</td>
        <td>248 -- 350</td>
        <td>2d</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ov126 @ 0x3935</code></td>
        <td>8</td>
        <td>0x01b6 -- 0x0962</td>
        <td>484 -- 2416</td>
        <td>map, message, model, pc_shop, script</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ov143 @ 0xd04</code></td>
        <td>33</td>
        <td>0x03ee -- 0x0470</td>
        <td>1007 -- 1137</td>
        <td>message</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The biggest table (180 records at <code>arm9 0x7ee5f</code>) covers
    14 different top-level folders — dialog, NPC data, scripts, models,
    and more all route through the same lookup mechanism. A few example
    entries from that table:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Content ID</th><th>File ID</th><th>Resolves to</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0xf37a</code></td><td>2550</td><td><code>talkstart/tkst00.ofs</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0xf37e</code></td><td>2555</td><td><code>talkstart/tkst05.ofs</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0xf37f</code></td><td>2560</td><td><code>talkstart/tkst10.ofs</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Talkstart files</h2>
  <p>
    The 40 talkstart files (<code>data/talkstart/tkstNN.ofs</code>) are
    the second hop in the chain. They are RESO v1.21 variant=1
    containers, and each entry inside one holds a header followed by an
    array of fixed 20-byte records. Each talkstart file represents a
    scenario type — greeting, shopping, combat, and so on — and each
    record within it is a specific slot for that scenario, identifying
    which character or zone is involved and which message file to load.
  </p>

  <h3>Entry header</h3>
  <p>Each entry begins with a 16-byte header:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Offset</th><th>Type</th><th>Field</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>+0x00</code></td><td>u32</td><td>record_count</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x04</code></td><td>u32</td><td>header_size (always 16)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x08</code></td><td>u32</td><td>unknown</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x0c</code></td><td>u32</td><td>total_entry_size (= 16 + record_count x 20)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Record type bytes</h3>
  <p>
    Each 20-byte record has a <strong>type byte at offset +0x12</strong>
    that determines how the rest of the record is interpreted. Three type
    bytes are classified:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Type byte</th><th>Meaning</th><th>File-ID field</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>0x02</code></td>
        <td>Dialog trigger</td>
        <td>u16 at <code>+0x04</code></td>
        <td>Loads and plays the message file at that file ID</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>0x0b</code></td>
        <td>Asset preload</td>
        <td>u16 at <code>+0x00</code></td>
        <td>Preloads a UI, sound, or 2D resource at scene start</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>0xff</code></td>
        <td>Data reference</td>
        <td>u16 at <code>+0x04</code></td>
        <td>Tracks an asset (<code>tkst20</code>--<code>31</code>) or indexes a per-scenario lookup (<code>tkst40</code>--<code>51</code>)</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Worked example: tkst20 entry 0</h3>
  <p>
    Entry 0 of <code>tkst20.ofs</code> is a verified chain. A selection
    of its records shows the pattern:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Record index</th><th>Resolves to</th><th>Content</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>0x1388 (5000)</td><td><code>magic/magic.lzs</code></td><td>Magic-list popup text</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0x138a (5002)</td><td><code>message/msg01/00/msg01001.ofs</code></td><td>Dialog file</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0x138b (5003)</td><td><code>message/msg20/04/msg20043.ofs</code></td><td>Dialog file</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0x13ab (5035)</td><td><code>message/msg98/05/msg98050.ofs</code></td><td>Dialog file</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Triggers 5002 through 5035 walk through roughly 36 different message
    files spaced exactly 32 file IDs apart. This is "play the same
    scenario but in a different NPC's voice" — one trigger per active
    speaker for that scenario.
  </p>

  <h2>Message files</h2>
  <p>
    The message files (<code>data/message/*.ofs</code>) are the terminal
    node of the chain — where actual dialog text lives. Each is a RESO
    v1.21 variant=1 container with up to 100 sparse slots. Each slot
    holds a dialog entry consisting of alternating CMD records (delimited
    by <code>0x2400</code> open / <code>0x3B00</code> close) and
    UTF-16-LE body text.
  </p>
  <p>
    The body text uses <code>U+0000</code> as phrase boundaries,
    a dedicated break marker for page/line breaks within a phrase, and
    <code>{`{kanji|reading}`}</code> notation for furigana. The full
    byte-level wire format is documented on the
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/message-system/wire-format/`}>wire format page</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Total across the game: 1,328 message containers holding 50,196
    dialog entries.
  </p>

  <h3>The message folder tree</h3>
  <p>
    The <code>data/message/</code> directory organizes its roughly 1,700
    files by category:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Subdirectories</th><th>Content</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>msg00</code> -- <code>msg12</code></td><td><code>/00/</code></td><td>System and event dialog</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg20</code> -- <code>msg31</code></td><td><code>/00/</code> through <code>/06/</code></td><td>Per-NPC personality dialog (12 personality types, roughly 45 files each)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg32</code> -- <code>msg33</code></td><td><code>/00/</code> through <code>/13/</code></td><td>Grouped dialog (NPCs by category)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg40</code> -- <code>msg52</code></td><td><code>/00/</code> through <code>/10/</code></td><td>Large groups (11 subdirs each) — classroom lessons, seasonal events</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg98</code></td><td><code>/00/</code> through <code>/09/</code></td><td>System messages, error text</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg99</code></td><td><code>/00/</code> through <code>/14/</code></td><td>UI labels, crafting tables, encyclopedia entries, lookup data</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Runtime rendering</h2>
  <p>
    Once the chain resolves to a message file and a slot within it,
    overlay 157 (the dialog renderer, always resident during
    conversations) takes over. The caller passes a <code>bubble_sel</code>
    byte that determines the rendering mode:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>0 = WINDOW</strong> — a 260 x 138 pixel dialog box at the
      bottom of the screen, typically three lines of roughly 30
      characters each.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>1--4 = BUBBLE</strong> — a 128 x 76 pixel floating bubble
      above the speaker, two lines maximum, with a practical cap of 32
      visible characters per page.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The renderer maintains a 660-byte instance struct per active dialog
    and runs a 9-state per-frame state machine to handle typewriter
    animation, color, and layout. The full rendering details are on the
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/message-system/bubble-vs-window/`}>bubble vs window page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Onemsg files</h2>
  <p>
    The 12 files in <code>data/onemsg/</code> are single-shot floating
    messages — the tooltip text that appears over characters' heads as
    the player walks past them. These always render as bubbles (32-char
    page cap) and use the same RESO v1.21 format as regular message
    files, but are loaded through a separate code path that bypasses
    talkstart resolution entirely. They enter the renderer with
    <code>bubble_sel = 4</code> hardcoded.
  </p>

  <h2>Open questions</h2>
  <p>
    Three parts of the chain are not yet fully traced:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The tokenizer function.</strong> The exact ARM function that
      parses the raw <code>0x2400</code>...<code>0x3B00</code> byte
      stream into the 28-byte records the dispatch consumes has not been
      identified. The strongest candidates are functions reachable from
      <code>arm9 @ 0x0200ba54</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Slot selection.</strong> A message file can hold up to 100
      slots. The logic that selects which slot to play for a given dialog
      trigger lives in the arm9 scene manager and has not been fully
      reverse-engineered.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Additional talkstart type bytes.</strong> Only type bytes
      <code>0x02</code>, <code>0x0b</code>, and <code>0xff</code> are
      classified. <code>tkst32</code> alone uses roughly 40 different
      type bytes whose meanings are unknown.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Related</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={`${base}/field-guide/message-system/wire-format/`}>Wire format</a> — the byte-level CMD + text encoding inside message files.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/field-guide/message-system/opcodes/`}>Opcode catalog</a> — all 88 CMD opcodes with hex values and arg layouts.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/field-guide/message-system/bubble-vs-window/`}>Bubble vs window</a> — how ov157 picks the rendering container and the 32-char bubble cap.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/field-guide/message-system/shared-payloads/`}>Shared payloads</a> — TOC deduplication within message containers.</li>
  </ul>
</FieldGuideLayout>
